\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Synthesis.}
VLA fine-tuning produces two training-induced representational changes---$R^2$ exit collapse and counterfactual sensitivity---that concentrate at the readout token (training shifts readout $R^2$ by $+0.069$ vs.\ image\_mean by $+0.015$).
L1 instruction attention is inherited from base-model pretraining (Phi-3-Vision: 32/32, Qwen2.5-VL: 13--14/16 before training).
The NLP control (Llama-2-7b-chat, $\Delta R^2 = 0.000$) confirms exit collapse is specific to action-oriented training.
VLA fine-tuning does not build instruction routing from scratch; it reorganizes downstream representations while preserving early-layer attention structure.

\paragraph{Cross-architecture consistency and behavioral relevance.}
Two VLM-to-VLA transitions converge on the same training-induced effects (6/6 cells, $p = 0.016$), compatible with task-structure constraints of causal-decoder VLA design.
OpenVLA confirms in a task-dependent manner under VLA-to-VLA conditions (2/4 cells).
The cross-architecture matrix suggests a three-tier gradient (VLM $\to$ general VLA $\to$ task-specific VLA), but Tier~2 contains only one architecture ($n = 1$), limiting this to an observational synthesis (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}).
Correlating trained $\Delta R^2$ with LIBERO success rates yields a positive but non-significant trend ($\rho = 0.583$, $p = 0.10$, $n = 9$); within-suite comparisons are uniformly positive but sample size limits statistical power (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}).

\paragraph{Limitations.}
Cross-architecture evidence rests on two VLM-to-VLA transitions; OpenVLA tests a different transition type.
All experiments use LIBERO; benchmarks with richer language grounding (CALVIN, Language-Table) are needed.
We analyze only causal-decoder VLAs; flow-matching~\cite{black2024_pi0} and diffusion~\cite{ghosh2024_octo} architectures may differ.
NORA lacks 2 of 4 suite checkpoints, and each architecture is analyzed at a single trained checkpoint.
Extended limitations appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}.
